WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.695
Welcome to UFI Career Studio. Ask stronger questions

00:00:03.695 --> 00:00:07.390
at the end. Use the closing minutes to

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.085
understand expectations, team priorities, and the next step

00:00:11.085 --> 00:00:14.780
in the process. We will work through five

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.475
practical steps, with examples you can adapt to

00:00:18.475 --> 00:00:19.861
your own experience.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.991
Ask how success will be judged in the

00:00:23.991 --> 00:00:27.316
first few months. A useful question is, What

00:00:27.316 --> 00:00:30.640
would you hope the person in this role

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.965
has accomplished after ninety days? Listen for specific

00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:37.290
outcomes and how they are measured. This helps

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.614
you understand the expectations behind the job description.

00:00:40.614 --> 00:00:43.939
If the answer is broad, ask which one

00:00:43.939 --> 00:00:46.432
or two priorities would come first.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.308
Find out what the team needs help with

00:00:50.308 --> 00:00:53.366
right now. Ask about a current challenge or

00:00:53.366 --> 00:00:56.424
an upcoming project, while respecting anything they cannot

00:00:56.424 --> 00:00:59.482
share. You can follow up with, What has

00:00:59.482 --> 00:01:02.540
made that difficult so far? This gives you

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.598
a more realistic picture of the work and

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:08.657
helps you decide whether your experience and interests

00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:10.186
fit the actual role.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.407
Explore how people work together day to day.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.813
Ask how feedback is given, how decisions are

00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:21.220
made, or how a new hire gets support.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.626
Choose questions that matter to your working style.

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:28.033
Rather than asking whether the culture is good,

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:31.439
invite an example of how the team handled

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:34.846
a recent change. Concrete examples are more informative

00:01:34.846 --> 00:01:37.400
than a list of positive adjectives.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.669
Before the conversation ends, you can ask whether

00:01:41.669 --> 00:01:45.088
there is anything about your experience that would

00:01:45.088 --> 00:01:48.506
be helpful to clarify. Keep the tone open

00:01:48.506 --> 00:01:51.925
rather than defensive. If they raise a concern,

00:01:51.925 --> 00:01:55.344
acknowledge it and respond with relevant evidence. If

00:01:55.344 --> 00:01:58.763
you have a genuine gap, explain how you

00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:02.181
would approach learning it. Do not invent experience

00:02:02.181 --> 00:02:03.891
just to remove uncertainty.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:08.172
Finish by thanking the interviewer and confirming the

00:02:08.172 --> 00:02:11.594
next step and expected timing. Prepare several questions

00:02:11.594 --> 00:02:15.015
in advance, but do not repeat one they

00:02:15.015 --> 00:02:18.437
already answered. For practice, choose two questions about

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:21.859
the work and one about the process. After

00:02:21.859 --> 00:02:25.281
the interview, write down what you learned while

00:02:25.281 --> 00:02:28.702
it is fresh so you can compare opportunities

00:02:28.702 --> 00:02:29.130
thoughtfully.
